00:01:49.000 --> 00:01:51.990
So not too long
ago, I was in Japan.

00:01:51.990 --> 00:01:56.150
And as anyone does in
Japan, they go get sushi.

00:01:56.150 --> 00:01:58.510
And I went to a pretty
unique sushi restaurant

00:01:58.510 --> 00:02:00.860
in the style of a conveyor
belt. And how it works

00:02:00.860 --> 00:02:01.905
is you take your seat.

00:02:01.905 --> 00:02:03.530
And there's this big,
rotating conveyor

00:02:03.530 --> 00:02:07.220
belt, where sushi chefs are
preparing sushi and placing it

00:02:07.220 --> 00:02:09.491
on the conveyor belt. And
eventually, it comes around

00:02:09.491 --> 00:02:10.699
to the table in front of you.

00:02:10.699 --> 00:02:12.620
And you could pick
it up and eat it.

00:02:12.620 --> 00:02:14.379
You could also order
specialty items,

00:02:14.379 --> 00:02:15.920
and the chefs will
make it, and it'll

00:02:15.920 --> 00:02:18.253
come around and make a little
sound when it's your item,

00:02:18.253 --> 00:02:19.250
and you can pick it up.

00:02:19.250 --> 00:02:22.989
And because I live and
breathe and eat monitoring,

00:02:22.989 --> 00:02:24.530
I started to think,
now how would you

00:02:24.530 --> 00:02:28.070
monitor this type of operation?

00:02:28.070 --> 00:02:30.020
And it got me thinking.

00:02:30.020 --> 00:02:33.560
And it made me realize that
this type of restaurant

00:02:33.560 --> 00:02:37.670
isn't too far off from a
website or a web server,

00:02:37.670 --> 00:02:41.300
where you're making requests for
sushi, and you get sushi back.

00:02:41.300 --> 00:02:43.160
And it made me think,
wow, this is really

00:02:43.160 --> 00:02:46.910
in parallel to how we think
about monitoring at Datadog.

00:02:46.910 --> 00:02:48.740
So how do we think
about monitoring?

00:02:48.740 --> 00:02:52.490
Well, we break it into
three unique parts.

00:02:52.490 --> 00:02:54.320
We have what's
called work metrics,

00:02:54.320 --> 00:02:57.320
and these are the set of
metrics that are directly

00:02:57.320 --> 00:02:59.310
impacting your end users.

00:02:59.310 --> 00:03:02.360
They are things like
throughput, successes, errors,

00:03:02.360 --> 00:03:04.100
how long things are taking.

00:03:04.100 --> 00:03:07.400
So if we think about
the sushi example,

00:03:07.400 --> 00:03:11.461
throughput might be how quickly
sushi is entering the conveyor

00:03:11.461 --> 00:03:11.960
belt, right?

00:03:11.960 --> 00:03:13.520
What's the number
of sushi pieces

00:03:13.520 --> 00:03:17.020
entering that conveyor
belt per second?

00:03:17.020 --> 00:03:19.520
What are the number of
correctly made sushi pieces

00:03:19.520 --> 00:03:21.180
versus incorrectly made?

00:03:21.180 --> 00:03:23.180
And finally, how long
does it take for customers

00:03:23.180 --> 00:03:26.360
to get that piece of sushi
after they ordered it?

00:03:26.360 --> 00:03:27.780
What's that latency?

00:03:27.780 --> 00:03:31.070
And again, these are those set
of metrics that are directly

00:03:31.070 --> 00:03:34.760
impacting your user experience
and ones that your user

00:03:34.760 --> 00:03:37.130
immediately notices.

00:03:37.130 --> 00:03:40.670
Under the hood, though, there's
a set of resource metrics.

00:03:40.670 --> 00:03:43.790
And these are the
things that are powering

00:03:43.790 --> 00:03:45.590
your system under the hood.

00:03:45.590 --> 00:03:48.500
So they're things like
utilization, saturation,

00:03:48.500 --> 00:03:50.200
errors, and availability.

00:03:50.200 --> 00:03:52.850
And to put this into
context, utilization

00:03:52.850 --> 00:03:55.550
might be how busy are
the chefs and how big

00:03:55.550 --> 00:04:00.050
is their backlog of items
that they have to prepare?

00:04:00.050 --> 00:04:02.745
How many pieces of the raw
ingredients do they have?

00:04:02.745 --> 00:04:03.620
Are they out of tuna?

00:04:03.620 --> 00:04:05.480
Are they out of rice?

00:04:05.480 --> 00:04:07.610
How much do they
have to work with?

00:04:07.610 --> 00:04:10.940
Customers might not notice
these immediately, right?

00:04:10.940 --> 00:04:12.770
They're not
customer-impacting metrics,

00:04:12.770 --> 00:04:16.560
but they very well might be
the root cause of issues.

00:04:16.560 --> 00:04:18.240
And finally, there's events.

00:04:18.240 --> 00:04:21.380
These are context-rich
sources of information

00:04:21.380 --> 00:04:24.380
that occur at a point in time.

00:04:24.380 --> 00:04:26.394
So I have those
examples, a code change.

00:04:26.394 --> 00:04:28.310
You might be configuring
something differently

00:04:28.310 --> 00:04:29.479
and push a change.

00:04:29.479 --> 00:04:31.020
You might spin up
new infrastructure.

00:04:31.020 --> 00:04:33.500
Any of these things
are events that

00:04:33.500 --> 00:04:36.270
might have an impact on
your operations, right?

00:04:36.270 --> 00:04:40.640
So a chef quitting, a knife
breaking, a machine breaking,

00:04:40.640 --> 00:04:45.920
any of these things could
impact your operations.

00:04:45.920 --> 00:04:50.300
So we put it into the
example of a modern system

00:04:50.300 --> 00:04:51.650
that you might recognize.

00:04:51.650 --> 00:04:55.490
We can look at RabbitMQ, which
is a popular, open source

00:04:55.490 --> 00:04:59.480
messaging system for sending
messages across systems.

00:04:59.480 --> 00:05:01.790
Those work metrics are
going to be the ones

00:05:01.790 --> 00:05:04.160
that your developers
or that your end users

00:05:04.160 --> 00:05:07.550
might actually notice, how big
is the queue of messages that

00:05:07.550 --> 00:05:09.800
are lining up, how many
unacknowledged messages are

00:05:09.800 --> 00:05:11.752
there, things along that nature.

00:05:11.752 --> 00:05:13.460
Under the hood, though,
there's something

00:05:13.460 --> 00:05:14.540
powering these queues.

00:05:14.540 --> 00:05:15.170
There's CPU.

00:05:15.170 --> 00:05:16.380
There's memory.

00:05:16.380 --> 00:05:18.329
There's network.

00:05:18.329 --> 00:05:20.120
There's compute resources
that are actually

00:05:20.120 --> 00:05:23.375
powering this queue system.

00:05:23.375 --> 00:05:25.500
And then, finally, there's
events that might occur.

00:05:25.500 --> 00:05:27.020
You might push code changes.

00:05:27.020 --> 00:05:29.300
You might update
configuration, all these things

00:05:29.300 --> 00:05:34.720
could affect the
performance of these queues.

00:05:34.720 --> 00:05:37.220
So great, we're collecting these
different types of metrics.

00:05:37.220 --> 00:05:39.580
We are looking at events.

00:05:39.580 --> 00:05:42.950
We're centralizing
it into one place.

00:05:42.950 --> 00:05:45.030
But we want to get notified
when things go wrong.

00:05:45.030 --> 00:05:47.530
We don't want to notice things,
on a dashboard, hours later.

00:05:47.530 --> 00:05:50.150
We want to get notified the
moment something arises,

00:05:50.150 --> 00:05:51.300
so that we can take action.

00:05:51.300 --> 00:05:55.230
So how should we think about
alerting and notification?

00:05:55.230 --> 00:05:57.770
So the way we think about
it is we want to page

00:05:57.770 --> 00:06:01.310
or get notified on those
work metrics, the metrics

00:06:01.310 --> 00:06:03.795
that our customers
are directly--

00:06:03.795 --> 00:06:06.080
that directly
affect the customer.

00:06:06.080 --> 00:06:07.300
The latency, right?

00:06:07.300 --> 00:06:10.340
The sushi is taking longer
to get to customers.

00:06:10.340 --> 00:06:12.920
Let's get alerted on that.

00:06:12.920 --> 00:06:15.410
The number of incorrect
pieces that our customers are

00:06:15.410 --> 00:06:16.460
receiving has doubled.

00:06:16.460 --> 00:06:18.440
Let's get alerted on that.

00:06:18.440 --> 00:06:20.180
Once you receive
those, you can then

00:06:20.180 --> 00:06:23.780
dig into those resource metrics
behind the hood, those ones

00:06:23.780 --> 00:06:26.882
that are powering the system,
and figure out what's going on.

00:06:26.882 --> 00:06:28.340
And then you could
also take a look

00:06:28.340 --> 00:06:30.800
at the events that have
occurred in that time frame.

00:06:30.800 --> 00:06:36.140
Maybe an event caused a change
that impacted your bottom line

00:06:36.140 --> 00:06:38.480
and affected your
customers directly.

00:06:38.480 --> 00:06:41.750
And the reason we think about
alerting on those work metrics,

00:06:41.750 --> 00:06:44.450
the ones that directly
impact the customer and not

00:06:44.450 --> 00:06:46.640
necessarily the
resource metrics,

00:06:46.640 --> 00:06:49.940
is the resource metrics don't
always tell you the full story.

00:06:49.940 --> 00:06:52.370
So if we think about
our sushi example,

00:06:52.370 --> 00:06:55.700
getting notified that the
amount of tuna we have in stock

00:06:55.700 --> 00:06:58.770
might be useful at 5:00
PM before the dinner rush.

00:06:58.770 --> 00:07:01.550
But at midnight
or 3:00 AM, you've

00:07:01.550 --> 00:07:03.170
just got paged
for something that

00:07:03.170 --> 00:07:06.980
might be unnecessary and
interrupt your sleep.

00:07:06.980 --> 00:07:12.050
So we like to think about
those customer-facing metrics.

00:07:12.050 --> 00:07:17.750
So let's talk about alerting in
the context of on-premise data

00:07:17.750 --> 00:07:20.120
centers.

00:07:20.120 --> 00:07:22.070
These data centers
are more static.

00:07:22.070 --> 00:07:26.870
You have a set of
servers in a data center,

00:07:26.870 --> 00:07:30.550
somewhere, that you've
allocated for your application.

00:07:30.550 --> 00:07:33.320
And when it comes to
alerting on those servers,

00:07:33.320 --> 00:07:36.220
it's a very
host-centric ideology.

00:07:36.220 --> 00:07:38.720
You know you might write these
checks or these notifications

00:07:38.720 --> 00:07:40.249
when a server is
having a problem.

00:07:40.249 --> 00:07:42.290
So in this example, we
have four servers running,

00:07:42.290 --> 00:07:44.480
and we have four
alerts that notify us

00:07:44.480 --> 00:07:49.130
when each of these
servers is going down.

00:07:49.130 --> 00:07:52.450
At a scale of four,
everything might be OK.

00:07:52.450 --> 00:07:54.060
We've written the checks.

00:07:54.060 --> 00:07:55.190
Everything's OK.

00:07:55.190 --> 00:07:56.780
But then what
happens if you want

00:07:56.780 --> 00:07:59.570
to spin up a new server, right?

00:07:59.570 --> 00:08:01.520
You've outgrown
your data center,

00:08:01.520 --> 00:08:02.990
and you've
architected a new one.

00:08:02.990 --> 00:08:07.740
Well then you have to add a
new alert for this server.

00:08:07.740 --> 00:08:09.116
So let's do that.

00:08:09.116 --> 00:08:11.210
But wait, actually,
we realize we

00:08:11.210 --> 00:08:13.250
need to spin up one
more server, and we

00:08:13.250 --> 00:08:17.870
need to add one more
server to our data center.

00:08:17.870 --> 00:08:19.660
So it's added an alert to that.

00:08:19.660 --> 00:08:21.770
And as you can see, as
you continue growing,

00:08:21.770 --> 00:08:25.520
this host-centric model becomes
an extremely manual process,

00:08:25.520 --> 00:08:27.200
because you have
to continue adding

00:08:27.200 --> 00:08:31.250
these host-centric
alerts to them

00:08:31.250 --> 00:08:33.380
in order to have full coverage.

00:08:33.380 --> 00:08:36.950
And what's more is you only
have that really host-centric

00:08:36.950 --> 00:08:38.600
information.

00:08:38.600 --> 00:08:40.614
You might get notified
that a host is down

00:08:40.614 --> 00:08:42.280
or a host is having
problems or a server

00:08:42.280 --> 00:08:44.900
is having problems,
but then what?

00:08:44.900 --> 00:08:46.760
That's only a small
sliver of the story.

00:08:46.760 --> 00:08:48.950
You might have an
entire environment,

00:08:48.950 --> 00:08:51.920
with databases and web
servers and who knows what,

00:08:51.920 --> 00:08:53.720
but you're only
getting notified when

00:08:53.720 --> 00:08:58.550
the server is having issues.

00:08:58.550 --> 00:09:00.140
But things are changing.

00:09:00.140 --> 00:09:04.010
We're moving into dynamic
infrastructures, where you're

00:09:04.010 --> 00:09:09.350
writing microservices across
regions and availability zones

00:09:09.350 --> 00:09:12.350
and using containers that spin
up and down and disappear.

00:09:12.350 --> 00:09:16.640
And you don't always know
what's running at one time.

00:09:16.640 --> 00:09:18.900
So how do you monitor
something like this,

00:09:18.900 --> 00:09:22.750
where it isn't static
and it's ever-changing?

00:09:22.750 --> 00:09:24.480
And the answer is tags.

00:09:24.480 --> 00:09:30.440
So tags are key value pairs that
provide context and information

00:09:30.440 --> 00:09:33.350
about all the different
pieces in your infrastructure.

00:09:33.350 --> 00:09:34.850
So if we look at
the Google console,

00:09:34.850 --> 00:09:37.740
right here, we have a
few machines running.

00:09:37.740 --> 00:09:40.220
And we'll notice a few sets
of important information

00:09:40.220 --> 00:09:42.240
that we consider tags.

00:09:42.240 --> 00:09:43.354
So we see a Zone.

00:09:43.354 --> 00:09:44.270
We see a Machine Type.

00:09:44.270 --> 00:09:45.240
We see a Network.

00:09:45.240 --> 00:09:48.500
And we see those labels that
are applied to each machine.

00:09:48.500 --> 00:09:52.730
All of these are
context-rich tags

00:09:52.730 --> 00:09:57.410
that give us information about
the infrastructure, itself.

00:09:57.410 --> 00:10:00.660
And what it lets us do
is create data points.

00:10:00.660 --> 00:10:03.912
So we can collect metrics from
all these various sources.

00:10:03.912 --> 00:10:05.870
So we can have a metric
that might be something

00:10:05.870 --> 00:10:06.786
like a bytes received.

00:10:06.786 --> 00:10:09.830
We can have a value, how many
bytes we actually received.

00:10:09.830 --> 00:10:10.970
When did it happen?

00:10:10.970 --> 00:10:14.090
And then we can add the
where did it come from.

00:10:14.090 --> 00:10:15.950
It came from a certain zone.

00:10:15.950 --> 00:10:18.930
It came from a certain
machine type, a hostname,

00:10:18.930 --> 00:10:22.280
a certain role, a
team, an environment.

00:10:22.280 --> 00:10:25.490
And then you can have context
around these data points,

00:10:25.490 --> 00:10:27.760
so that you can do
things like this.

00:10:27.760 --> 00:10:30.650
Now I want you to imagine,
each one of these cubes

00:10:30.650 --> 00:10:33.620
has some data point
in it, a number that

00:10:33.620 --> 00:10:35.950
could be the number of errors.

00:10:35.950 --> 00:10:41.930
On each cube is a set of
tags that describe that cube.

00:10:41.930 --> 00:10:45.590
It's a combination of a machine
type, a zone, and a role.

00:10:45.590 --> 00:10:48.260
And these are all machines
that might be bundled up.

00:10:48.260 --> 00:10:51.860
And what you can do is start
to group, splice and dice,

00:10:51.860 --> 00:10:55.010
and filter this data in a
way that's important to you

00:10:55.010 --> 00:10:56.327
based on these tags.

00:10:56.327 --> 00:10:58.910
So, for instance, if we wanted
to get those three purple cubes

00:10:58.910 --> 00:11:00.980
across the middle,
we'd say, OK, show

00:11:00.980 --> 00:11:09.110
me all of the data for
us-east1-a and machine type

00:11:09.110 --> 00:11:10.520
highmem-2.

00:11:10.520 --> 00:11:13.860
And it can return you all the
metrics for that exact criteria

00:11:13.860 --> 00:11:14.360
of data.

00:11:16.801 --> 00:11:18.050
And what does this let you do?

00:11:18.050 --> 00:11:22.340
Well, one, it lets you move
from a host-centric model

00:11:22.340 --> 00:11:26.180
of alerting to service-centric
model of alerting.

00:11:26.180 --> 00:11:27.680
And I want you to
imagine a scenario

00:11:27.680 --> 00:11:33.170
where you have a group of
Google Compute Engines that all

00:11:33.170 --> 00:11:34.940
have some common tag.

00:11:34.940 --> 00:11:35.670
Say it's zone.

00:11:35.670 --> 00:11:37.070
These are all in a similar zone.

00:11:37.070 --> 00:11:39.590
And right now, we
have one alert that's

00:11:39.590 --> 00:11:44.060
monitoring all of the
machines in this one zone.

00:11:44.060 --> 00:11:47.970
So if you spin up a
new group of instances,

00:11:47.970 --> 00:11:50.360
say, you autoscale up,
you need more capacity,

00:11:50.360 --> 00:11:53.180
and those machines
are in the same zone,

00:11:53.180 --> 00:11:56.060
well, your alert
already covers that.

00:11:56.060 --> 00:11:58.460
It's looking for that
zone tag and saying,

00:11:58.460 --> 00:12:00.710
I'm alerting on all
things in that zone.

00:12:00.710 --> 00:12:03.260
If new hosts get spun
up with that same zone,

00:12:03.260 --> 00:12:05.220
I can automatically
alert on them.

00:12:05.220 --> 00:12:08.300
You don't need to manually
add any new alerts.

00:12:08.300 --> 00:12:10.130
There's nothing to configure.

00:12:10.130 --> 00:12:12.510
Alerting just works.

00:12:12.510 --> 00:12:16.520
And if we go back to our
small example of RabbitMQ,

00:12:16.520 --> 00:12:18.350
and we look at it
quickly in Datadog,

00:12:18.350 --> 00:12:21.585
this is an example of creating
that type of alert in Datadog.

00:12:21.585 --> 00:12:23.210
And what we're looking
at is the number

00:12:23.210 --> 00:12:26.270
of messages coming from
each one of our queues.

00:12:26.270 --> 00:12:28.670
And it's only looking
in one particular zone,

00:12:28.670 --> 00:12:30.110
that us-central zone.

00:12:30.110 --> 00:12:32.120
So we have four queues there.

00:12:32.120 --> 00:12:33.080
It's split up by name.

00:12:33.080 --> 00:12:35.270
And if any of the
number of messages,

00:12:35.270 --> 00:12:38.570
in any of these queues,
reaches 10, we get alerted.

00:12:38.570 --> 00:12:42.650
So if we create a new queue
in zone us-central1-b,

00:12:42.650 --> 00:12:45.630
it'll automatically applied
to this alert group.

00:12:45.630 --> 00:12:50.280
And you can get notified
and take action right away.

00:12:50.280 --> 00:12:54.110
So that's a brief
foray into monitoring,

00:12:54.110 --> 00:12:58.090
what we think about monitoring,
how we approach monitoring,

00:12:58.090 --> 00:13:01.584
and how monitoring is changing
as infrastructure is changing.

00:13:01.584 --> 00:13:03.250
I'm now going to pass
it off to Michael,

00:13:03.250 --> 00:13:05.666
who's going to talk more about
Marketo and their migration

00:13:05.666 --> 00:13:07.512
journey.

00:13:07.512 --> 00:13:09.827
[APPLAUSE]

00:13:09.827 --> 00:13:11.680
MICHAEL MCCLINTOCK: Thank you.

00:13:11.680 --> 00:13:12.561
Can you all hear me?

00:13:12.561 --> 00:13:13.060
Yep.

00:13:13.060 --> 00:13:13.870
Great.

00:13:13.870 --> 00:13:14.680
So I'm Michael.

00:13:14.680 --> 00:13:18.580
I am a senior systems
administrator with Marketo.

00:13:18.580 --> 00:13:20.440
I'm working out of
our Dublin office

00:13:20.440 --> 00:13:22.570
in EMEA, hence the funny accent.

00:13:22.570 --> 00:13:24.150
I work with Marketo.

00:13:24.150 --> 00:13:27.410
And we are a customer
engagement platform.

00:13:27.410 --> 00:13:30.460
So what we do is we do
marketing automation.

00:13:30.460 --> 00:13:32.230
And I'm not a
salesman, so if you

00:13:32.230 --> 00:13:33.550
want to know more about
customer engagement

00:13:33.550 --> 00:13:35.050
and how Marketo can
help you improve

00:13:35.050 --> 00:13:36.740
the way you engage
with your customers,

00:13:36.740 --> 00:13:38.420
we have a stand
right over there.

00:13:38.420 --> 00:13:41.080
So you have no excuse not to go
on your way out of this talk.

00:13:41.080 --> 00:13:43.330
What's really important on
this slide, that you should

00:13:43.330 --> 00:13:46.150
have a look at, is that we
grew by 30% with our revenue

00:13:46.150 --> 00:13:47.700
in 2017.

00:13:47.700 --> 00:13:50.770
And so that caused us
some scaling issues.

00:13:50.770 --> 00:13:53.355
And so we encountered
the on-premise issues

00:13:53.355 --> 00:13:54.730
that you will have
heard at talks

00:13:54.730 --> 00:13:56.760
right across this
conference this week.

00:13:56.760 --> 00:14:00.450
And so we made a decision
to partner with GCP

00:14:00.450 --> 00:14:03.749
and to move our
infrastructure to Google.

00:14:03.749 --> 00:14:05.290
So the sort of issues
we were having,

00:14:05.290 --> 00:14:07.780
that we were looking to
fix with a move to GCP

00:14:07.780 --> 00:14:11.284
were scalability issues
and flexibility issues.

00:14:11.284 --> 00:14:13.450
But we were also having
some issues with monitoring.

00:14:13.450 --> 00:14:16.600
So while we investigated
and embarked

00:14:16.600 --> 00:14:18.820
on a journey to
move to GCP, we also

00:14:18.820 --> 00:14:22.032
decided to change the way that
we monitor our applications.

00:14:22.032 --> 00:14:24.490
So the sort of issues that we
were having on the monitoring

00:14:24.490 --> 00:14:27.190
side is that we
had multiple tools

00:14:27.190 --> 00:14:28.510
to monitor the same things.

00:14:28.510 --> 00:14:29.920
So we had different messages.

00:14:29.920 --> 00:14:31.330
We had different thresholds.

00:14:31.330 --> 00:14:34.210
We had different teams, who
were seeing different noise

00:14:34.210 --> 00:14:36.540
from different monitoring tools.

00:14:36.540 --> 00:14:38.720
And that didn't work for us.

00:14:38.720 --> 00:14:42.820
And so what we decided to do was
try and group everyone together

00:14:42.820 --> 00:14:45.290
and start using Datadog.

00:14:45.290 --> 00:14:53.320
So what we began
with, in our GCP move,

00:14:53.320 --> 00:14:56.380
is that we had to make a
decision on how to move

00:14:56.380 --> 00:14:59.570
our infrastructure to GCP.

00:14:59.570 --> 00:15:01.720
And so we had this
sort of discussion,

00:15:01.720 --> 00:15:04.280
that I'm sure everyone
will have considered,

00:15:04.280 --> 00:15:06.430
which is that we either
would take a lift and shift

00:15:06.430 --> 00:15:08.800
approach, which was to
make our infrastructure,

00:15:08.800 --> 00:15:10.990
within GCP, work
for our application,

00:15:10.990 --> 00:15:12.820
or we'd take a
transformational approach

00:15:12.820 --> 00:15:16.350
and make our application
work within GCP.

00:15:16.350 --> 00:15:19.210
And there's loads and loads of
upsides and downsides to this,

00:15:19.210 --> 00:15:19.710
right?

00:15:19.710 --> 00:15:21.980
There is no silver bullet.

00:15:21.980 --> 00:15:23.644
There's no objectively
correct answer.

00:15:23.644 --> 00:15:25.810
And what we decided to do
is we would lift and shift

00:15:25.810 --> 00:15:30.650
our application as the first
phase of our migration.

00:15:30.650 --> 00:15:33.670
And so we embarked
on phase one, which

00:15:33.670 --> 00:15:37.390
has now been completed as part
of a three-phase migration

00:15:37.390 --> 00:15:38.150
to GCP.

00:15:38.150 --> 00:15:39.550
So next year, when
you come back,

00:15:39.550 --> 00:15:42.149
we can talk a bit more
about the other two phases.

00:15:42.149 --> 00:15:44.440
And as part of that, we also
got to work really closely

00:15:44.440 --> 00:15:45.020
with GCP.

00:15:45.020 --> 00:15:47.176
So we partnered with Google.

00:15:47.176 --> 00:15:48.550
And we got to work
with their CRE

00:15:48.550 --> 00:15:50.980
team, which has been fantastic.

00:15:50.980 --> 00:15:54.040
And we got to get
Google staff on-site

00:15:54.040 --> 00:15:56.320
to tell us a little bit of
best practices of running

00:15:56.320 --> 00:15:58.409
applications in the cloud.

00:15:58.409 --> 00:16:00.700
If you want to know more
about the CRE transition story

00:16:00.700 --> 00:16:03.390
that we did, tomorrow,
in the 5th Nine Lounge,

00:16:03.390 --> 00:16:07.160
which is just
downstairs, on level 2,

00:16:07.160 --> 00:16:10.950
we are going to have a talk
from Cindy Kim just about that.

00:16:10.950 --> 00:16:15.040
So why did we go with Datadog?

00:16:15.040 --> 00:16:19.000
Well, as we started moving away
from our own infrastructure,

00:16:19.000 --> 00:16:21.160
it made sense for us to
take our monitoring out

00:16:21.160 --> 00:16:22.580
of our infrastructure, as well.

00:16:22.580 --> 00:16:26.087
And so a SaaS-based
solution worked for us.

00:16:26.087 --> 00:16:28.420
We also were able to take
advantage of those preexisting

00:16:28.420 --> 00:16:30.720
integrations within Datadog.

00:16:30.720 --> 00:16:34.510
And so we were able to get
rich, deep metrics about GCP

00:16:34.510 --> 00:16:36.850
from day one, without
needing our engineers

00:16:36.850 --> 00:16:38.530
to go in and create
those metrics

00:16:38.530 --> 00:16:41.464
and start working out
ways to record that.

00:16:41.464 --> 00:16:43.630
And also, we really, really
liked the customer check

00:16:43.630 --> 00:16:45.520
writing within Datadog.

00:16:45.520 --> 00:16:48.280
So the agent, itself,
is Python-based

00:16:48.280 --> 00:16:50.860
and checks can be
written pretty simply.

00:16:50.860 --> 00:16:53.110
And because you have
access to all of the checks

00:16:53.110 --> 00:16:55.600
that they provided, you get
some really good best practice

00:16:55.600 --> 00:16:58.070
in the code that's shipping
and that you're deploying

00:16:58.070 --> 00:17:00.530
to your service already.

00:17:00.530 --> 00:17:03.280
So what we decided to
do, when we pushed it,

00:17:03.280 --> 00:17:05.859
is we used Puppet to push it
across our infrastructure.

00:17:05.859 --> 00:17:08.390
You could push Datadog,
the agent, itself,

00:17:08.390 --> 00:17:10.270
you could push it within a day.

00:17:10.270 --> 00:17:12.260
I don't know if you
should, but you could.

00:17:12.260 --> 00:17:14.440
You could do it, because
all you really have to do

00:17:14.440 --> 00:17:16.117
is push the agent and
start the service.

00:17:16.117 --> 00:17:17.950
And as long as you've
got the API key there,

00:17:17.950 --> 00:17:20.380
you'll see your
metrics, straightaway.

00:17:20.380 --> 00:17:22.599
And that was really,
really useful for us.

00:17:22.599 --> 00:17:24.579
It was able to give
us a situation where

00:17:24.579 --> 00:17:27.160
we could do a proof of concept
with Datadog really, really

00:17:27.160 --> 00:17:28.657
quickly.

00:17:28.657 --> 00:17:29.740
And then what we did was--

00:17:29.740 --> 00:17:31.906
I'll talk a little bit about
how easy it was later--

00:17:31.906 --> 00:17:34.660
but we configured
our Google Cloud

00:17:34.660 --> 00:17:36.580
integration within
Datadog, which

00:17:36.580 --> 00:17:39.700
allowed us to start getting
information about resources

00:17:39.700 --> 00:17:42.130
that we were using within GCP.

00:17:42.130 --> 00:17:44.560
And then we started
the arduous journey

00:17:44.560 --> 00:17:47.980
of converting our existing
monitoring solution

00:17:47.980 --> 00:17:51.790
and moving our infrastructure,
our monitoring into Datadog,

00:17:51.790 --> 00:17:55.499
which involved moving
over 1,000 Nagios checks.

00:17:55.499 --> 00:17:57.790
And if you have questions
about how to go through that,

00:17:57.790 --> 00:18:00.123
absolutely come up at the
end, and we can talk about it.

00:18:00.123 --> 00:18:02.560
Because it's fun.

00:18:02.560 --> 00:18:04.186
We introduced Google
Spanner, as well.

00:18:04.186 --> 00:18:05.560
And this is a
really good example

00:18:05.560 --> 00:18:08.770
of where the Google integration
helped us, straightaway.

00:18:08.770 --> 00:18:14.770
So Google Spanner was something
that we started using.

00:18:14.770 --> 00:18:16.540
And thanks to Datadog,
we were able to get

00:18:16.540 --> 00:18:19.330
deep metrics of
how Spanner works

00:18:19.330 --> 00:18:22.369
and what we were doing in our
usage of Spanner, straightaway.

00:18:22.369 --> 00:18:24.160
So even though we were
lifting and shifting

00:18:24.160 --> 00:18:28.930
all of our applications, on
any part of our application

00:18:28.930 --> 00:18:30.730
where we decide to do
some transformation,

00:18:30.730 --> 00:18:32.890
we were able to use Datadog,
straightaway, and not

00:18:32.890 --> 00:18:35.990
have to worry
about these changes

00:18:35.990 --> 00:18:39.050
as we iterated our application.

00:18:39.050 --> 00:18:42.350
So here's an example
of-- or we could just

00:18:42.350 --> 00:18:44.880
talk quickly about
how you would monitor

00:18:44.880 --> 00:18:47.690
a migration with Datadog.

00:18:47.690 --> 00:18:50.930
So thanks to what Daniel was
talking about, with tagging,

00:18:50.930 --> 00:18:54.440
we were able to effectively
monitor our old infrastructure

00:18:54.440 --> 00:18:56.480
and our new infrastructure,
side by side,

00:18:56.480 --> 00:19:01.430
and quickly group by whether a
server is in GCP, for example,

00:19:01.430 --> 00:19:03.650
or what version of
code we're running

00:19:03.650 --> 00:19:09.260
or which, I guess, zones and
geographical information, as

00:19:09.260 --> 00:19:10.080
well.

00:19:10.080 --> 00:19:12.470
And what that meant was that,
pretty much, from day one,

00:19:12.470 --> 00:19:14.720
if you're taking a lift
and shift approach,

00:19:14.720 --> 00:19:16.790
you're able to see
how the performance

00:19:16.790 --> 00:19:20.420
of your application, within GCP,
matches with your performance

00:19:20.420 --> 00:19:21.360
on-prem.

00:19:21.360 --> 00:19:22.910
And that's really
useful information,

00:19:22.910 --> 00:19:23.990
because you can find
out if you're doing

00:19:23.990 --> 00:19:25.590
something wrong pretty quickly.

00:19:25.590 --> 00:19:27.080
And you can also
find out if it's

00:19:27.080 --> 00:19:29.389
way better than
you were expecting.

00:19:29.389 --> 00:19:30.930
And here's an example
of a dashboard.

00:19:30.930 --> 00:19:33.380
So you're also able to
provide, to your engineers,

00:19:33.380 --> 00:19:36.740
pretty much, very
simply a side by side

00:19:36.740 --> 00:19:40.310
view of your
application's performance.

00:19:40.310 --> 00:19:42.905
Any code changes they push,
they can see that straightaway.

00:19:42.905 --> 00:19:47.140
And it's really easy to
design, so even I can do it.

00:19:47.140 --> 00:19:50.690
So other changes
that we implemented

00:19:50.690 --> 00:19:54.470
or other initiatives that we
took, as part of this move, we

00:19:54.470 --> 00:19:57.029
decided we'd go down an
infrastructure-as-code route,

00:19:57.029 --> 00:19:58.820
which meant that we
started using Terraform

00:19:58.820 --> 00:20:01.940
to control our GCP environment.

00:20:01.940 --> 00:20:03.540
But we didn't want
to stop there.

00:20:03.540 --> 00:20:06.590
We wanted to try and use this
philosophy or this approach

00:20:06.590 --> 00:20:08.660
everywhere that we could.

00:20:08.660 --> 00:20:11.900
So there is actually a Terraform
provider available for Datadog.

00:20:11.900 --> 00:20:16.490
And what that means is that
you can control your Datadog

00:20:16.490 --> 00:20:18.920
instance with Terraform,
which is great.

00:20:18.920 --> 00:20:21.859
Because one of the big concerns
that we had, and, certainly,

00:20:21.859 --> 00:20:24.150
if you're considering Datadog,
a concern you might have

00:20:24.150 --> 00:20:26.150
is that, if you have
a SaaS solution, which

00:20:26.150 --> 00:20:28.220
is fully managed
through a web client,

00:20:28.220 --> 00:20:30.780
you're worried about who can
access it, who can change it.

00:20:30.780 --> 00:20:33.380
What happens if you
lose your configuration?

00:20:33.380 --> 00:20:35.730
What happens if you
click the wrong thing?

00:20:35.730 --> 00:20:37.160
If you manage this
with Terraform,

00:20:37.160 --> 00:20:39.860
you've got some
really good options.

00:20:39.860 --> 00:20:43.510
So, for example, you could just
lock down the entire web client

00:20:43.510 --> 00:20:46.880
and make it impossible to manage
the instance through the web

00:20:46.880 --> 00:20:49.500
client and make all the
changes go through Terraform.

00:20:49.500 --> 00:20:51.350
And what that then
allows you to do

00:20:51.350 --> 00:20:53.900
is you can put all
of your changes

00:20:53.900 --> 00:20:56.780
to your Datadog environment
through code review.

00:20:56.780 --> 00:21:00.870
It effectively acts as
a DR system, as well.

00:21:00.870 --> 00:21:02.610
If you lose your
Datadog instance,

00:21:02.610 --> 00:21:05.520
you can rebuild
it pretty quickly.

00:21:05.520 --> 00:21:10.070
And it allows you to roll back
any bad monitors you introduce,

00:21:10.070 --> 00:21:14.320
anything that's too noisy, or
anything that isn't working.

00:21:14.320 --> 00:21:17.780
And as part of this
change, we also

00:21:17.780 --> 00:21:20.130
changed the way that we
handle incident response.

00:21:20.130 --> 00:21:23.865
So this is an ongoing move
that we're making still.

00:21:23.865 --> 00:21:26.490
I mean, you could have the best
monitoring system in the world,

00:21:26.490 --> 00:21:28.660
but it's kind of useless
if it's not paging you

00:21:28.660 --> 00:21:30.470
or if it's paging
you in the wrong way.

00:21:30.470 --> 00:21:32.553
And so we started working
with Pagerduty, as well,

00:21:32.553 --> 00:21:34.180
as part of this move.

00:21:34.180 --> 00:21:38.420
And that allowed us to have
a rules-based, really deep,

00:21:38.420 --> 00:21:43.680
powerful pager routing system.

00:21:43.680 --> 00:21:47.810
And it allowed us to populate
and pass our Datadog tags

00:21:47.810 --> 00:21:49.640
all the way to our
on-call engineer.

00:21:49.640 --> 00:21:52.430
So as Daniel was
pointing out, the tags

00:21:52.430 --> 00:21:54.920
were one of the most
valuable bits of information

00:21:54.920 --> 00:21:58.640
that we were able to add
into our observability.

00:21:58.640 --> 00:22:03.480
So we were able to take
a tag from our servers--

00:22:03.480 --> 00:22:08.081
so for example, the version
of code that it's running,

00:22:08.081 --> 00:22:09.830
the role that the
server is playing within

00:22:09.830 --> 00:22:11.000
the application--

00:22:11.000 --> 00:22:12.500
and when we page
an on-call engineer

00:22:12.500 --> 00:22:16.610
or we wake somebody up, we have
that information right there.

00:22:16.610 --> 00:22:18.570
And it's available to everyone.

00:22:18.570 --> 00:22:22.220
And so this allowed us
to really improve the way

00:22:22.220 --> 00:22:25.400
that we were
conveying information

00:22:25.400 --> 00:22:27.200
to our engineers
who were on-call.

00:22:27.200 --> 00:22:28.840
And on-call is not
an easy thing to do

00:22:28.840 --> 00:22:31.070
or it's not a nice place
to be, so it's always

00:22:31.070 --> 00:22:34.560
nice to make it as
easy as possible.

00:22:34.560 --> 00:22:37.370
So just as an example
of the sort of ease

00:22:37.370 --> 00:22:39.260
of use and quality
of life improvements

00:22:39.260 --> 00:22:44.680
that you get out of using
Datadog as part of this,

00:22:44.680 --> 00:22:48.360
the Google Cloud integration
is really simple to configure.

00:22:48.360 --> 00:22:51.370
You can do it in
minutes, I would say.

00:22:51.370 --> 00:22:54.110
So all you really need to do
is you create a service account

00:22:54.110 --> 00:22:55.307
within GCP.

00:22:55.307 --> 00:22:57.140
And you just make sure
that you're giving it

00:22:57.140 --> 00:22:58.610
the correct permissions.

00:22:58.610 --> 00:23:00.140
I think we have a slide here.

00:23:00.140 --> 00:23:01.089
It's hard to read.

00:23:01.089 --> 00:23:03.380
But as long as you've got
monitoring viewer permissions

00:23:03.380 --> 00:23:06.500
within that service
account, that account

00:23:06.500 --> 00:23:08.480
can pretty much start
giving your information

00:23:08.480 --> 00:23:11.210
into your Datadog instance,
straightaway, on day one.

00:23:11.210 --> 00:23:14.450
So you just take a
JSON-based private key,

00:23:14.450 --> 00:23:17.590
and then you go into
your Datadog instance,

00:23:17.590 --> 00:23:19.310
and you just put
that key in place.

00:23:19.310 --> 00:23:23.210
And you will start getting
information, straightaway.

00:23:23.210 --> 00:23:28.410
And that comes with pre-built
dashboards, so within 15,

00:23:28.410 --> 00:23:31.220
20 minutes of starting
to use Datadog and GCP,

00:23:31.220 --> 00:23:34.100
you can start seeing all of your
resources, all of your usage,

00:23:34.100 --> 00:23:37.340
everything you need on a
dashboard, straightaway.

00:23:37.340 --> 00:23:41.810
And this is an example
of the integrations

00:23:41.810 --> 00:23:45.180
that you get within Datadog.

00:23:45.180 --> 00:23:47.270
So these are the
GCP integrations

00:23:47.270 --> 00:23:50.420
that they provide dashboards and
monitoring for, out of the box.

00:23:50.420 --> 00:23:54.620
But what's really useful is
that it doesn't stop here.

00:23:54.620 --> 00:23:57.650
So Daniel said there's
250-plus integrations, which

00:23:57.650 --> 00:23:59.234
means any
application-- if you're

